Source offer for jig

jig is written in bash and Python, so the package of each release is its own
source: the files you install are the files that run.

Each release of jig is published with two archives on the same release page
of the jig repository:

- jig-<version>.tar.gz, the package, which holds the tree of the release
  commit;
- jig-<version>-src.tar.gz, the source archive, which holds the same files
  under the directory jig-<version>/ and builds and tests with make, with no
  network access.

The tag v<version> in the jig repository names the commit of each release, and
the repository holds the history of the source from its first published commit
on. Both archives stay on the release page for as long as the release is
published.

jig contains no code of others; the programs it runs at run time are listed
in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES, and their source comes from their own vendors.
